Results FX sampler (JOED-63)

The read-only sampler uses its dedicated paper client 709 to request
reqAccountUpdatesMulti(id, ACCOUNT, "", true). It collects ExchangeRate values
for the configured account until accountUpdateMultiEnd and always cancels the
request before disconnecting. It makes no FX market-data request. EUR must be
exactly 1 to prove an EUR base; the positive USD decimal rate is padded or
rounded half-even to eight decimal places using BigInt.

IB updates account values periodically. This is IB's valuation rate as received
at sampleAt, not a simultaneous executable FX quote. sampleAt records receipt
of the USD account value, before accountUpdateMultiEnd. The producer carries
that timestamp unchanged as observedAt.

staleFx: true and integer staleSeconds appear together once the compared string
has stayed identical for at least 1800 seconds (30 minutes), measured from the
earliest sample in the contiguous identical run of that New York day's samples
to the new RTH or close sample's sampleAt, rounded down to seconds. A different
rate resets the run; failed attempts do not change it. Below 1800 seconds both
keys are absent. This is informational: the rate is still used for conversion.

ibRate stores IB's raw USD ExchangeRate string exactly as received: a positive
decimal of at most 32 characters, without leading zeros except the integer 0.
The stale clock compares ibRate when both samples have it, so even a change
beyond eight decimal places resets the run; otherwise it compares rate for
older samples. ibRate is informational; conversion still uses the eight-place
half-even rate.

session: "off" appears only on the fixed 16:05 New York close sample of an
NYSE early-close day, after the 13:00 close. Normal-day close and all RTH
samples omit session. Staleness is still computed on the off-session row.

New samples carry source "ib-account-exchange-rate". The producer labels their
conversions "ib-account-fx-close" or "ib-account-fx-rth". Historical samples
without source retain "ib-fx-close" or "ib-fx-rth". A frozen 16:05 New York
close wins for its day; an RTH sample expires after 30 minutes. A fill's own
closeFx keeps its existing 60-second freshness requirement.

Slots, the 15-minute gap, unconditional close, one attempt per bucket, timeout,
reserved request IDs, retention and FX-SAMPLER-OFF are unchanged. Failed IB
requests may record an integer code with the existing reason. Broker messages
and account IDs are never logged or persisted by the sampler. Old state files
without source or code remain readable; unknown sample sources fail closed.

Executor health (JOED-198)

GET /v1/health on 8470 and the Results loopback listener on 8472 give the
ledger queue 100 ms to start a fresh read. While a job holds the queue, both
listeners can return HTTP 200 with stale: true and the latest ledger snapshot.
The snapshot refreshes before each claim, on ledger publication, and when a
capture failure is retained in memory. queueWaitMs measures the probe's wait
and queueHeldMs measures how long the active job has held the queue. Stale
replies include the UTC observation time (snapshotAt) and monotonic age
(snapshotAgeMs). Both listeners sample live HALT and ledger usage after the
queue wait and gateway probe finish. Capture admission diagnostics use that
live usage. An unverified ledger generation yields a degraded reply.

Both listeners return HTTP 503 with reason ledger_queue_stalled, queueHeldMs,
and jobKind when the active job has gone more than 120 seconds without
progress. Intents mark broker session attempts and completions, and mark
progress between orders and positions during cancellation and flattening.
In ib.mjs a session attempt has a 20-second timeout, contract and FX lookups
allow 10 seconds each, and quote snapshots and acknowledgements take up to
4 seconds per wait. The 120-second no-progress limit gives those bounded
steps and local ledger work room to finish. Sequential recon replay attempts
each renew the deadline.

A missing snapshot also returns HTTP 503. A failed health read returns an
HTTP error and logs one value-free health_error code until health recovers.
When the shared fresh read starts, it writes one value-free health_queue_wait
line with waitedMs from the first timed-out probe's arrival, the jobKind
that held the queue, and the staleReplies count for the episode. Timed-out
probes detach from the shared read.

The gateway TCP probe runs in parallel and can take up to two seconds. Keep
the watchdog's client timeout comfortably above 2.1 seconds.
Synchronous disk or CPU work can delay the Node event loop and its timers.
